INSERT INTO episode (slug, number, title, blurb, style, audio_key, duration_s, session_number) VALUES ('the-master-and-the-disciple', 1, 'Three Thanks and the Persian Awakening', 'Episode one of *The Master and the Disciple* opens on a group of the truly faithful and a group of du''at al-din standing in front of one of their scholars with three thanks owed at once — for the call, for the knowledge, for the practice — and asking how on earth they are supposed to repay them. The Master answers first with a metaphysics of stages: every beginning has been given an end, every end a goal, every goal a limit whose full extent cannot be reached. He names the three-stage spiritual path balwa-huda-taqwa, and only then names the three deeds — obedience for the call, action for the knowledge, perseverance for the practice. The episode then enters the story of the Persian seeker, the man whose thirst for knowledge led him through mirage after mirage until he reached the place of nothing and met Allah there. From that meeting he came under the same proverb that had once been his master''s: ''The most excellent of deeds is giving life to the dead.'' His interior monologue runs the chain backward — did the trust really descend from the heavenly host through every intermediary only to stop with him? The answer requires action. So he sets out across the Arab and non-Arab lands, and the episode lands with him at the far edge of al-jazira, the disciple still unfound.
', 'deep_dive', 'the-master-and-the-disciple/audio/ep01.mp3', 991, 1);
INSERT INTO episode (slug, number, title, blurb, style, audio_key, duration_s, session_number) VALUES ('the-master-and-the-disciple', 2, 'The Test of Speech and the Design of Creation', 'Episode two of *The Master and the Disciple* picks up the Persian scholar at the far edge of al-jazira. He enters a city where nobody knows him, sits out of sight to listen to a group of townspeople debate religion without a guide, and picks them — for three precise reasons — as deserving of life: the hujja has reached them; they belong to the milla; and they are already inquiring. They test his speech, and he passes the test by refusing authorship: ''I have no words of my own, for I am following the words of another.'' Recognized as a gateway, he rises and delivers the great design-of-creation sermon, the book''s central cosmology, in which the outer and the inner are named as a deliberate mechanism of preservation rather than a textual layer. The episode then turns to the instrument by which the listener uses the chain — hearing, sight, and the heart, named together in four Quranic verses as gift, as sealed, as covered, and as the very things by which Allah will hold the listener to account. The episode lands on the youngest of the group following the scholar to his inn — the disciple chosen.
', 'deep_dive', 'the-master-and-the-disciple/audio/ep02.mp3', 1005, 1);
INSERT INTO episode (slug, number, title, blurb, style, audio_key, duration_s, session_number) VALUES ('the-master-and-the-disciple', 3, 'Five Conditions and the Covenant', 'Episode three of *The Master and the Disciple* delivers the structural climax of the opening narrative arc. The young man has begged to be raised; the scholar answers with the infant analogy and the graduated ladder of spiritual learning. Before he agrees to teach a single doctrine, he sets the precondition: knowledge is of no benefit until it has been put into action, and action is of no benefit until it is accepted, and the only thing Allah accepts is sincerity. He then imposes five conditions — and the fifth reveals the political stake: the boy''s father is a known leader of a rival community that has rejected the Imam, and premature disclosure of the da''wa could destroy the mission. The conditions are protocols of taqiyya, not pedagogy. After a period of trial, the meeting is arranged. The scholar names the key of religion — the ''ahd Allah — and delivers the analogy: marriage and fornication are the same act, distinguished by the nikah; halal and haram are the same animal, distinguished by the dhabiha; sacred and stolen are the same religious practice, distinguished by walaya. Imam Ja''far al-Sadiq''s apple-and-charity teaching locks the doctrine: a stolen apple given to charity propagates the haram and earns ten sins, not forty-five rewards. The episode lands on the title-teaches move — disciple from discipline, discipline from shari''a — and on the night of the oath, the boy''s tears, his certainty of belonging to the Hizb Allah.
', 'deep_dive', 'the-master-and-the-disciple/audio/ep03.mp3', 1125, 1);
INSERT INTO episode (slug, number, title, blurb, style, audio_key, duration_s, session_number) VALUES ('the-master-and-the-disciple', 4, 'Originator and the Three Primordial Words', 'Episode four of *The Master and the Disciple* begins the doctrinal core of the book with the architecture of creation laid down with mathematical precision. The scholar starts with what kind of First Allah is — Originator without template, first of every first, inheritor of all existence. He names the three primordial words that fall from the first Light: will, command, speech — densifying as they descend. From those three words come seven principles: air the unpaired first and king, then water, darkness, and physical light, then their denser pairs of smoke, mud, and fire. From smoke come the seven heavens; from mud the seven earths. And the letters give the numerical signature: seven in the command kun fa-yakun, twelve in the three primordial words — stamped into the cosmos as seven heavens, seven oceans, seven days; twelve zodiacs, twelve regions, twelve hours of day and twelve of night. The signature of cosmology turns out to be the letter-count of its own genesis.
', 'deep_dive', 'the-master-and-the-disciple/audio/ep04.mp3', 1001, 2);
INSERT INTO episode (slug, number, title, blurb, style, audio_key, duration_s, session_number) VALUES ('the-master-and-the-disciple', 5, 'Bismillah Seal and the Chosen Ranks', 'Episode five of *The Master and the Disciple* shows how the architecture of creation is sealed into the Bismillah — a cryptographic lock whose three names and letter-counts mirror the three primordial words and the sevens-and-twelves of cosmology, point for point. Then it names who fills the houses of prophethood: distinguished personalities and guiding figures by category and function — Natiq, Imam, Bab, Hujaj, Nuqaba, Du''at, ulama. And it mirrors the hierarchy in the visible sky: the Imam is the sun, his Bab the moon, the Du''at the stars. A finer distinction is preserved that listeners often miss: the twelve regional Hujaj are proofs, but the Bab is the one Gateway — the Hujja al-Imam, the single moon to the one sun.
', 'deep_dive', 'the-master-and-the-disciple/audio/ep05.mp3', 535, 2);
INSERT INTO episode (slug, number, title, blurb, style, audio_key, duration_s, session_number) VALUES ('the-master-and-the-disciple', 6, 'Zahir, Batin, and the Seven Natiqs', 'Episode six of *The Master and the Disciple* names the seven speaking prophets — Adam, Noah, Abraham, Moses, Jesus, Muhammad, Mahdi — and shows how the calendar of cosmology (sevens, twelves, thirties) is the calendar of spiritual transmission. Then it gives the deepest framing of the whole architecture: zahir and batin are not two textual layers but the body and the spirit of religion, each subsisting only through the other. Religion is the soul of the revealed paths; the ordinances of shari''a are the body. Each lives through the other; neither lives without the other. Finally the scholar answers the disciple''s question about the world by walking the visible cosmos as a catalogue of symbols: the heaven points to the Natiq; the zodiac to the Nuqaba; the earth to the Bab and Wasi; the islands to the Hujaj; the rivers and springs to the Du''at. And the salty sea encodes outward knowledge while the sweet spring encodes inner knowledge — both are water, both are life, but only one feeds the field.
', 'deep_dive', 'the-master-and-the-disciple/audio/ep06.mp3', 1076, 2);
INSERT INTO episode (slug, number, title, blurb, style, audio_key, duration_s, session_number) VALUES ('the-master-and-the-disciple', 7, 'Air and the Instance Beyond Air', 'Episode seven of *The Master and the Disciple* takes the architecture of creation to its lofty edge. One element of the cosmology has been waiting since the very first concept: Air — the first of the seven, the unpaired king, what mixes with everything and is encompassed by nothing. The disciple asks about it. The scholar refuses to make air a symbol of any creation, then derives — through three nested scale comparisons (a seal-ring on a plain, a seal-ring in a desert, an egg on a plain) — why air points only upward. The disciple raises a Quranic objection that seems to topple the whole teaching: do not strike likenesses for Allah. And the scholar turns it into the doctrine''s strongest pillar with a second verse from the same chapter: and Allah''s is the loftiest similitude. Both verses are true. Lesser signs are forbidden as peers in a series; the loftiest similitude is licensed because it is the sign that announces the failure of signs. Then the disciple pushes one rung further, and the scholar names what stands behind even Air: al-Awan, the Instance of His divine will, the doorway of irada itself. The disciple pleads to be brought back from the lofty edge. The scholar invokes Moses and the scholar — Khidr — and closes the dialogue at its lofty edge, leaving the inner of the inner unopened.
', 'deep_dive', 'the-master-and-the-disciple/audio/ep07.mp3', 805, 2);
INSERT INTO episode (slug, number, title, blurb, style, audio_key, duration_s, session_number) VALUES ('the-master-and-the-disciple', 8, 'Three Levels of Knowledge — Angels, Humans, and Beasts', 'Episode eight of *The Master and the Disciple* opens Part Three by turning the architecture inside out. The disciple asks whether the inner has an inner, and the scholar answers yes — there is an outer, an inner, and an innermost of that inner aspect, and the triple structure is everywhere in Allah''s book and in the very grammar of how anything is understood. The verse of the garment carries it. The grammar of name, attribute, and meaning carries it. The shell, white, and yolk of an egg carries it. And then the scholar maps the three layers of knowledge onto three levels of creatures: the beasts, who know only the outer; the humans, who know the inner; and the angels, who know the inner of the inner — at whose rung sit the prophets, Allah''s vicegerents on earth. The disciple asks the question that follows: can the highest rung be climbed from below? The scholar answers with the farmer who cannot make the plant grow and with the rope of Allah held tightly through the Imam — reached through his proofs and his gateway. The disciple completes the move himself: there is no power and no strength except through Allah. The next episode will open that formula and show what has been waiting inside it.
', 'deep_dive', 'the-master-and-the-disciple/audio/ep08.mp3', 1037, 3);
INSERT INTO episode (slug, number, title, blurb, style, audio_key, duration_s, session_number) VALUES ('the-master-and-the-disciple', 9, 'Hawl and Quwwa, and the Dream of Joseph', 'Episode nine of *The Master and the Disciple* takes apart the line every Muslim recites in difficulty: there is no power and no strength except through Allah. The disciple offers the common readings — hawl as "a year," quwwa as "mules" — and the scholar walks them into absurdity before opening the inner meaning. Hawl is the Imam who converts the Creator''s speech into the words of wisdom that human beings can hear; quwwa is the Bab who bears the divine speech in its condensed, all-inclusive form and distributes it to the Du''at in the form each can carry. The formula is the confession of an entire chain that is itself dependent at every link. Then the same architecture is read out of two of the great dreams of Surat Yusuf — Joseph''s eleven stars and the sun and the moon (chapter 12, verse 4) and the king''s seven fat cows and seven green ears — and the cosmology of Part Two is heard inside Part Three''s formulae and dreams.
', 'deep_dive', 'the-master-and-the-disciple/audio/ep09.mp3', 808, 3);
INSERT INTO episode (slug, number, title, blurb, style, audio_key, duration_s, session_number) VALUES ('the-master-and-the-disciple', 10, 'The Union of Inner and Outer', 'Episode ten of *The Master and the Disciple* answers the question every reader of esoteric doctrine must ask. If the inner is everything, what becomes of the outer — of prayer, of fasting, of the daily obligations of shari''a? The scholar answers in three movements. First the union of inner and outer as a single living religion: the seeker who keeps only the inner is fruit rotting before ripening; the one who keeps only the outer is a body without spirit, numbered among the dead; only their union is faith, and for the believer this world becomes licit. Second the pale faces of the true scholars: not a refusal of the world but the body''s record of vigilant attention to the next, distracted from luxury by reflection and engrossed in the sweetness of Allah''s remembrance. And third the doctrine of divine justice in acceptance: the instrument of knowledge is identical in every chest; the difference is in what each seeker does with it. The episode closes with mercy under necessity, told through the thirsting woman in the time of the second caliph and the Father of Imams'' ruling that the verse of necessity left her free of sin.
', 'deep_dive', 'the-master-and-the-disciple/audio/ep10.mp3', 919, 3);
INSERT INTO episode (slug, number, title, blurb, style, audio_key, duration_s, session_number) VALUES ('the-master-and-the-disciple', 11, 'Purifying Possessions and Parting from the Master', 'Episode eleven of *The Master and the Disciple* closes the dialogue between scholar and disciple. The disciple''s question is the most concrete he has asked: what do I do with what I own? The scholar gives a rule — lawful acquisition; zakat from the whole; khums from the finest portion you love most; the rest for family with the surplus to the needy. The disciple surprises him by having already divided his possessions into five portions with pedagogical weights — zakat; atonement kept as remembrance of sins; gratitude held as a trust until maturity; brothers'' portion given by his own hand; and a portion to support the scholar. The scholar weeps and refuses his own portion, redirecting each of the others and turning the purification portion into a trust for the Imam — named here as the rightful judge whose hands hold the keys of the gardens and whose palms reveal the light of Sinai. Then the scholar travels to consult the Sheikh, his own spiritual father, who instructs him to hurry back because the young man is to become a gateway of loving kindness in his own time. And when the scholar returns he finds the disciple pale and weak with longing — ishq, the source names it, extreme love at separation from the beloved. The scholar announces the journey, and the disciple sighs so deeply with joy and awe that his spirit almost leaves his body.
', 'deep_dive', 'the-master-and-the-disciple/audio/ep11.mp3', 916, 3);
INSERT INTO episode (slug, number, title, blurb, style, audio_key, duration_s, session_number) VALUES ('the-master-and-the-disciple', 12, 'Rebirth of the Disciple and the Seven-Day Tawaf', 'Episode twelve of *The Master and the Disciple* opens Part Four. The disciple and the scholar reach the Sheikh. The Sheikh begins with a sustained praise of Allah — justice as the balance between command and prohibition, the honor of intellect as its use in the search, the duties of truth as obligatory, the signs to be shown in the cosmos and in the self. Then he turns to the disciple and refuses every name offered until the disciple has no answer left. A name, he says, can only be given after seven days, in honor of the new-born infant; the universe itself received its life from the seventh letter, and the embryo passes through seven stages before another creation is brought forth. On the seventh day, after ablution and a fresh garment, the disciple is drawn close, taught the realities pens cannot write, and brought into a tawaf the source reads as the planets orbiting their sun — the Imam as the centre, gravity as love, marifa as the orbit in which the planet''s life is held.
', 'deep_dive', 'the-master-and-the-disciple/audio/ep12.mp3', 777, 4);
INSERT INTO episode (slug, number, title, blurb, style, audio_key, duration_s, session_number) VALUES ('the-master-and-the-disciple', 13, 'Homecoming and the Forty-Year Syllogism', 'Episode thirteen of *The Master and the Disciple* sends the disciple home. The Sheikh releases him for three reasons — his own travel, the father''s sorrow, the disciple''s new obligation to transmit — and binds him with four counsels: be mindful of Allah, uphold the oath, safeguard the scholar at his side, keep his identity concealed. At the outskirts of the city the scholar names his residence and gives six parting counsels that read as a da''i''s operating manual. Resolution entrusted to Allah; words of wisdom in calling; no anger at being wronged; no upset at being called a liar; patience with the responder; being the best example. The disciple thanks him for three things — the Imam whose argument was upheld, the hujja whose trust was kept, the corpse to which life was given — kisses his feet, and goes home to a father whose charge has two horns: you concealed the truth from me, or you destroyed yourself by deception. The disciple meets the syllogism with a syllogism. Over forty years either you were a scholar who withheld, or you were ignorant who should have preceded me. Every door closes. The father weeps and enters the path; through father and son a great number of people are revived.
', 'deep_dive', 'the-master-and-the-disciple/audio/ep13.mp3', 510, 4);
INSERT INTO episode (slug, number, title, blurb, style, audio_key, duration_s, session_number) VALUES ('the-master-and-the-disciple', 14, 'Abu Malik Arrives and the Fair Inquiry', 'Episode fourteen of *The Master and the Disciple* brings the champion of the opposing community on stage. The former co-religionists of al-Bakhtari, who are afraid of losing his favors and afraid of being on the wrong side of his conversion, send for the Yemeni scholar Abu Malik — Kab al-Ahbar of his age, the glory of the yearned. He responds with striking fairness. He refuses to condemn the two converts without hearing them and refuses to assume their guidance without testing it; both, he says, would be injustice. He warns his own companions against the satisfaction of past communities who said no messenger would come after theirs and did not see the way of proceeding beyond what they had attained. He gives the conversion three possible readings — decorative addition; real increase of knowledge as the believer''s lost camel; or true prophetic guidance — under each of which the right move is to go and meet. He names the inversion of signs by which falsehood wears conformity and truth wears trial, and defines the sound heart as the one that does not deny what enters it. He insists on humility before the truth, not after. And he refuses delay: today, not in three days, not in two. The greatest scholar of the opposing camp has just set the terms of his own debate by the standard of justice.
', 'deep_dive', 'the-master-and-the-disciple/audio/ep14.mp3', 1011, 4);
INSERT INTO episode (slug, number, title, blurb, style, audio_key, duration_s, session_number) VALUES ('the-master-and-the-disciple', 15, 'Emptying the Cup — The Demolisher''s Art', 'Episode fifteen of *The Master and the Disciple* opens the central debate of Part Four. Abu Malik arrives at al-Bakhtari''s door and is met by a threefold riddle that re-arranges every hierarchy he carries with him — the son is the father; the Lord is on earth with a servant; the mortal lives forever. Abu Malik mistakes it for news of death and recites the verse for the deceased. Inside the house, Salih receives him with perfect manners and prays for the ears of his heart to be opened. Then Salih asks which of three kinds of seeker he is — the one who knows, the one beginning to know, or the one who desires to know. Abu Malik admits he does not know the modes of seeking at all and places himself among the beginners. The source steps back and names the technique: kasrah, demolition; the rank in the spiritual hierarchy that performs it is the mukasir, the Demolisher. Then Salih draws the distinction that will govern everything that follows: ilm versus marifa, knowledge versus gnosis, khabar versus iyan, report versus vision. The alim knows heaven and hell exist; the arif burns to reach the one and avoid the other. Abu Malik confesses he found that intense neediness in his own soul. Salih cautions him against rushing the rebuilding; every seed requires its ground to be prepared, and every knowledge requires a heart first purified by proper argument.
', 'debate', 'the-master-and-the-disciple/audio/ep15.mp3', 1013, 4);
INSERT INTO episode (slug, number, title, blurb, style, audio_key, duration_s, session_number) VALUES ('the-master-and-the-disciple', 16, 'Jewels, the Moon, and the Description of Religion', 'Episode sixteen of *The Master and the Disciple* closes the great debate of Part Four. Abu Malik invites Salih to state his arguments under the image of wisdom as jewels in the sunlight; Salih accepts the image with one condition — it holds only where there is no confusion between real jewel and forged glass. The story of the jewelers'' verdict on his former faith leads to the principle that spiritual knowledge must be presented to those who receive it from the heavens before it can be deemed genuine. Abu Malik asks who, after the prophets, such people can be; Salih answers by refusing the standing of any religion grounded only in transmitted reports. The example is moon-sighting. The hadith of moon- sighting has turned, Salih says, into the hadith of moon-fighting. The Messenger''s instruction "follow the moon" was the designation of the wasi the community was to follow — the moon as the established symbol, the wasi who follows the sun''s path — and the community collapsed the symbol into the disc. The Quran''s own description of the sun and moon on fixed calculated paths witnesses against the chaos. A divorce case study — a man left not knowing whether he is married or divorced — brings Abu Malik to the confession that sharia has been corrupted. Salih dissolves the religion=sharia equivalence by which Abu Malik had been holding what was left and gives his own description of religion: the veil, the rope from the heavens, the firm handhold, the ark of the divine presence, the ship of salvation, the light of life, the well- protected knowledge, the jewels with their distinctive signs. Abu Malik accepts the description as the essential meaning religion would have to have and asks for the reality of which the description is the picture. The way is opened for the final part of the debate.
', 'debate', 'the-master-and-the-disciple/audio/ep16.mp3', 1126, 4);
INSERT INTO episode (slug, number, title, blurb, style, audio_key, duration_s, session_number) VALUES ('the-master-and-the-disciple', 17, 'Who Is Allah? — Beyond Names and Adjectives', 'Episode seventeen of *The Master and the Disciple* opens the climax of the debate. Salih refuses to let the conversation about Allah''s justice and unity happen at the level of the school''s standard creed. Names cannot cook food. The four letters of the divine name are signs, not containers. The analogical proof from manufactured things secretly forges a likeness. The standard formula ''nothing like His image'' even smuggles an image back under the table. The eye that cannot bear the sun cannot weigh the Creator. By the end Abu Malik says it himself: his words have left him; intellects are bewildered; imaginations cannot reach Him. That confession is the doorway into the next episode''s syllogism.
', 'debate', 'the-master-and-the-disciple/audio/ep17.mp3', 935, 5);
INSERT INTO episode (slug, number, title, blurb, style, audio_key, duration_s, session_number) VALUES ('the-master-and-the-disciple', 18, 'The Syllogism of Divine Justice', 'Episode eighteen of *The Master and the Disciple* delivers the syllogism the whole book has been building toward. Allah cannot be sensed. Allah cannot be unjust. Therefore a chain of just witnesses must extend Allah''s command to creatures who could not receive it otherwise. Their existence is Allah''s act of justice. The chain has rank-order in function but equality in reward. The trustee carries the esoteric interpretation as a second channel of the same divine knowledge that placed the outer book. The chain cannot be cut without converting Allah''s blessing into the withdrawal of the means of worship — which divine justice forbids. Abu Malik affirms it as the very foundation of justice.
', 'debate', 'the-master-and-the-disciple/audio/ep18.mp3', 1125, 5);
INSERT INTO episode (slug, number, title, blurb, style, audio_key, duration_s, session_number) VALUES ('the-master-and-the-disciple', 19, 'The Conspiracy Formula', 'Episode nineteen of *The Master and the Disciple* delivers the book''s most distinctive piece of social analysis — the conspiracy formula. Three actors in every period — a rebellious devil, a willing oppressor, and a hypocritical religious scholar — collaborate to sever the outward traces of prophecy from its living continuation so that the traces can be exploited as a commodity. The slogan that holds the severance in place is ''no prophet after our prophet,'' propagated until inquiry itself becomes redundant. Salih traces the pattern across the Zoroastrian, Torah, and Gospel communities, names this community as the latest case, and turns the consensus argument back: by that very logic, the earlier communities hold the greater claim. The Creator''s freedom to abrogate and confirm voids every closure argument anyway.
', 'debate', 'the-master-and-the-disciple/audio/ep19.mp3', 1142, 5);
INSERT INTO episode (slug, number, title, blurb, style, audio_key, duration_s, session_number) VALUES ('the-master-and-the-disciple', 20, 'Unbroken Chain and the Path of Return', 'The final episode of *The Master and the Disciple*. Salih refutes the very notion of an interval between prophets by naming the chain link by link, and rereads the Quranic word fatra as a period of fear in which the call is made in secret rather than as an absence of the witness from the earth. The concealment of the friends of Allah in the listener''s own time is explained by the way of Moses fleeing into Midian. The three categories of people — relentless murderer, rageful enemy, sympathetic abandoner — locate the moral weight not on the enemies but on those who feel for the witnesses but abandon them. The scholars who legitimate the oppressors are named as worse than the murderers themselves. The path of return is brief and exact: help the friends of Allah and they will reveal themselves; abandon their enemies and they will not be victorious. Abu Malik repents. The source seals the book by declaring its arc a replicable model from individual conversion to community-building. The closing praise-and-blessings seal closes the work.
', 'debate', 'the-master-and-the-disciple/audio/ep20.mp3', 883, 5);
