INSERT INTO episode (slug, number, title, blurb, style, audio_key, duration_s, session_number) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 1, 'Knowledge Without Action', 'The opening of Imam al-Ghazali''s letter *Ayyuha al-Walad* — the diagnosis half of his reply to a long-time disciple who wrote asking which of the many sciences he had been taught would actually save him on the Day of Judgment. Ghazali refuses the assumption that more teaching is the deficit (*if you have not received benefit from the counsels of the Prophet, then tell me — what knowledge have you truly acquired from me?*) and presses a single verdict from seven angles: the Prophet''s three warnings on useless knowledge and the forty-year clock and the scholar''s severest torment; the dream of Shaykh Junaid Baghdadi whose lifetime of discourse weighed nothing while a small number of voluntary Tahajjud prayers were of benefit; the lion-in-the- jungle parable and the unswallowed medicine; the Quranic verses on the atom''s weight and the verses of faith joined to action; the hadith that Islam is *built upon* five pillars and that faith is testimony-tongue-heart-and-action-of-limbs; the careful clarification that this is not works-righteousness but the condition of receiving Mercy (*the Mercy of Allah is close to the doers of good*); and the story of the worshipper among the Children of Israel whose unconditional service drew the forgiveness the angel''s test had pretended to withhold. The episode closes on the Prophetic hinge — *take account of yourselves before you are taken to account* — and the three closing witnesses on acting without self-admiration. The lion is coming; the swords are in the hand; whether the arm will swing is the work of the next episode.
', 'deep_dive', NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO episode (slug, number, title, blurb, style, audio_key, duration_s, session_number) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 2, 'The Path of Obedience and Struggle', 'The second half of the opening movement of *Ayyuha al-Walad* — the pivot from diagnosis to cure. Ghazali turns the disciple from the verdict that knowledge alone will not save him to the inhabited practice of the dawn watch: Hasan al-Basri''s involuntary sigh at the cold cup; the standing pre-dawn proclamation *is there anyone who repents, anyone who asks of Me, anyone who seeks My forgiveness?*; the Quranic command addressed directly to the Prophet ﷺ to perform *Tahajjud*; the four angels of the four stations of the night; the wind Allah has created that carries the voices of His servants at the hour before dawn; and Luqman''s testament to his own son not to let the rooster prove more intelligent than he. From the dawn watch Ghazali turns to the principle that worship is another name for obedience to the Prophetic *Shari''ah*, and works it out through four edge-cases — the fast on Eid as sin, the prayer at a forbidden hour as transgression, the prayer on unlawfully occupied ground, and the lawful intimacy between husband and wife that the uninformed would judge as appetite — to establish that the Prophetic frame is the test of whether any act is worship at all. He then turns to the inner condition: the Path cannot be travelled by accumulated knowledge, only by *mujahadah*, and the lower self must be severed with the sword of struggle. The polemic against the simulation of Sufism — and the parenthetical defense of its true practice — closes that movement. The episode ends on Ghazali''s first answer to the disciple''s second question: the four conditions of the sincere seeker, sealed by Shibli''s hadith of the four proportions which contains *the knowledge of all who came first and all who came last*. The disciple now knows what watch to keep, what rule to follow, what struggle to wage, and what four conditions frame the life.
', 'deep_dive', NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO episode (slug, number, title, blurb, style, audio_key, duration_s, session_number) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 3, 'The Shaykh and the Disciple''s Rule of Life', 'The practical rule of life Imam al-Ghazali hands his disciple. Three braided strands. First, the eight benefits Hatim ibn Ism brought back to his master Shaykh Shafeeq al-Balkhi from long inward observation of the people of the world — each one anchored in a Quranic verse, each one the same shape: *I looked, I weighed against the Book, I changed what I did.* The only beloved who descends into the grave is one''s own righteous deeds; the Nafs must be disciplined under Quran 79:40–41; wealth must be sent ahead under Quran 16:96; Taqwa is the only true nobility under Quran 49:13; contentment with the assigned portion under Quran 43:32; Shaytan alone is the enemy under Quran 35:6 and the covenant verse; sustenance is Allah''s responsibility under Quran 11:6; Tawakkul on the One who suffices under Quran 65:3. Shaykh Shafeeq''s verdict on the eight is that whoever acts on them has acted on all four sacred Books — Tawrat, Injil, Zabur, Quran. Second, the figure at the center of the seeker''s life — the Shaykh al-Kamil, the perfected guide whose Bay''a chains unbroken back to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ; the full catalog of his qualifications; the bright line that he cannot command anything forbidden by the Shari''ah; the inner etiquette of no doubt in the heart — with the honest-withdrawal clause when the inward and outward cannot agree. Third, the inner vocabulary of the Path — Ghazali''s brief, precise definitions of Tasawwuf (Shari''ah + good toward creation), servitude (obey, accept, dissolve the *I want*), Tawakkul (certitude in what is promised), and Ikhlas (deeds for Allah alone, with the metaphysical cure for Riya). The chapter closes with Ghazali''s instruction that the rest of the answers come through practice rather than further questioning — three Quranic verses on not hastening, the verb *travel* from Quran on those who refuse to set out, and Shaykh Zun Noon Misri''s line that the path is the soul actually offered, not the beautiful sayings of the Sufis. The eight admonitions belong to the next episode.
', 'deep_dive', NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO episode (slug, number, title, blurb, style, audio_key, duration_s, session_number) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 4, 'Eight Admonitions and a Closing Prayer', 'The master''s closing brief to his beloved disciple. Eight admonitions, structured as four things to refrain from and four things to actively attend to — so that knowledge does not turn against the scholar on the Day of Judgment. The four refrainings: do not engage in religious debate (with the two narrow signs of right intention if the rare licit argument arises, and the long aside on the four categories of patients — only the obedient, sharp, intelligent seeker is worth answering); do not preach (with the *Ya Ibna Maryama* address to Jesus and the *Al-Hazr! Al-Hazr!* flood image governing what a sermon must be — fear, the Hereafter, Munkar and Nakir, the Mizan, the Sirat — and the *summoning of God''s creation from this toward that* litany governing what the preacher''s heart must seek); do not greet or even look upon kings, the wealthy, or rulers (with the hadith that praying for an oppressor''s long life is, in effect, praying for the prolongation of oppression); and do not accept their gifts even from lawful sources (with the *Shaytan*-counsel about routing money through the poor as the lure itself). The four takings-up: an intimate relationship with Allah audited by the household-servant analogy; right relating to the slaves of Allah audited by the sacred hadith *my slave''s faith is never complete until he loves for all human beings what he loves for himself*; focused study under the one-week-to-live thought experiment and the king-is-visiting house-cleaning image anchored by *Allah does not look at your faces or your outward actions, but at your hearts and your intentions*; and not accumulating more than one year''s provision, with the Prophet ﷺ''s calibrated practice for the Azwaj al-Mutahharat and ''Aisha Siddiqa. The chapter closes with the long supplication the disciple is to recite after every Salaat — the abundance block, the correction block, and the eleven divine names sealing the letter — and with the master''s final naming of Al-Malik and Al-Wahhab as the help under which the book has been written. The letter ends here.
', 'deep_dive', NULL, NULL, NULL);
